  participation signals.

  Last semantic question before I can propose architectures. "When tasks they care about change" — the event taxonomy
  determines what the event log stores and how much volume the system generates.

  The fork is how fine-grained an event is, which is really a question about whether notifications are [3mper-change[0m or
  [3mdigested[0m.

  - [1mEvery field change is an event[0m — status, title, due date, description, assignee, each its own notification. Complete
    audit trail, and the activity feed comes free. But a person editing a task for two minutes generates eight
    notifications, so this [3mrequires[0m batching/coalescing to be usable. That batching logic is real work.
  - [1mA curated set of notable events[0m — status change, assignment, comment, and nothing else. Title and description edits
    are silent. Far less volume, no batching needed initially, and it covers what people actually want to know. Cost:
    the audit trail is incomplete, and "notable" is a judgment you'll revisit.
  - [1mCoarse "task updated"[0m — one event type, collapse everything. Trivial to build. Also close to useless — a
    notification that doesn't say what changed makes people open the task to find out, every time.

  My recommendation is [1mthe curated set[0m, specifically status / assignment / comment. It's the honest 80% and it lets us
  ship without building a coalescing engine. The event log can still record every change for audit purposes while only a
  subset is [3mnotifiable[0m — that separation costs nothing now and means widening the set later is a config change, not a
  redesign.
